<?php
// Read input
$line = trim(fgets(STDIN));
list($n, $t) = explode(' ', $line);
$n = (int)$n;
$t = (int)$t;

// We need to count sequences y1, y2, ..., yn where:
// - each yi is in {1, 2, 3, 4}
// - no two consecutive yi are equal (no segment parallel to Ox-axis)
// - exactly t peaks: indices j (2<=j<=n-1) such that y[j-1] < y[j] > y[j+1]
// - exactly t-1 valleys: indices j (2<=j<=n-1) such that y[j-1] > y[j] < y[j+1]

// Since n <= 20 and yi in {1,2,3,4}, we can use DP.
// State: position i, last value y[i], whether the previous segment was going up or down,
// and count of peaks and valleys so far.
//
// Actually, let's think more carefully.
// A peak at position j (2<=j<=n-1) requires y[j-1] < y[j] > y[j+1].
// A valley at position j requires y[j-1] > y[j] < y[j+1].
//
// We can build the sequence from left to right. At each step i (from 1 to n), we choose y[i].
// The constraints on peaks/valleys depend on triples (y[i-1], y[i], y[i+1]).
//
// So when we are at position i and have chosen y[i], we can't yet determine if position i-1 is a peak or valley until we choose y[i+1].
//
// Let's use DP where state is:
// dp[i][last][prev_dir][peaks][valleys]
// where i is the current position (1-indexed), last is y[i], prev_dir is the direction from y[i-1] to y[i] (up or down),
// peaks and valleys are the counts of peaks and valleys confirmed so far.
//
// But actually, when we add y[i], we can confirm whether position i-1 is a peak or valley based on y[i-2], y[i-1], y[i].
//
// Let's redefine:
// dp[i][a][b][p][v] = number of ways to have a sequence of length i, where y[i-1]=a, y[i]=b,
// and we have p peaks and v valleys among positions 2..i-1 (i.e., positions where the triple is fully determined).
//
// Wait, let's be more precise. When we have chosen y[1], y[2], ..., y[i], the positions 2 through i-1 have their triples determined (since we have y[j-1], y[j], y[j+1] for j=2..i-1).
//
// So:
// dp[i][a][b][p][v] = number of valid sequences of length i ending with y[i-1]=a, y[i]=b,
// with exactly p peaks and v valleys in positions 2..i-1.
//
// Base case: i=2. We have y[1]=a, y[2]=b, a != b. No peaks or valleys yet (positions 2..1 is empty).
// So dp[2][a][b][0][0] = 1 for all a != b, a,b in {1,2,3,4}.
//
// Transition: from dp[i][a][b][p][v], we choose c = y[i+1], c != b.
// Now we can determine if position i is a peak or valley:
// - If a < b > c: position i is a peak. New peaks = p+1, valleys = v.
// - If a > b < c: position i is a valley. New peaks = p, valleys = v+1.
// - Otherwise: neither. New peaks = p, valleys = v.
//
// Then dp[i+1][b][c][p'][v'] += dp[i][a][b][p][v].
//
// Final answer: sum over all a, b, p, v of dp[n][a][b][p][v] where p == t and v == t-1.
//
// Constraints: n <= 20, a,b in {1,2,3,4}, p <= 10, v <= 10.
// This is very manageable.

$dp = [];

// Initialize for i=2
for ($a = 1; $a <= 4; $a++) {
    for ($b = 1; $b <= 4; $b++) {
        if ($a == $b) continue;
        $dp[2][$a][$b][0][0] = 1;
    }
}

// Build up to i=n
for ($i = 2; $i < $n; $i++) {
    for ($a = 1; $a <= 4; $a++) {
        for ($b = 1; $b <= 4; $b++) {
            if ($a == $b) continue;
            for ($p = 0; $p <= $t; $p++) {
